## Why This Is Asked
Examines practical isolation controls for logs in multi-tenant cloud-native systems, including data-minimization, policy enforcement, and incident response.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped logging
- ABAC/OPA policy enforcement at ingestion
- Data redaction and least-privilege exporters
- SPIRE/mTLS between log agents and collectors
- Ephemeral credentials rotation and secrets hygiene

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactForTenant(log, tenantId) {
  const redacted = {...log};
  const sensitiveKeys = ['ssn','creditCard','dob','email'];
  for (const k of sensitiveKeys) {
    if (redacted[k] && redacted.tenantId === tenantId) {
      redacted[k] = '[REDACTED]';
    }
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for cross-tenant access in search results?
- What monitoring signals indicate redaction failures or policy drift?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply multi-tenant isolation, data governance, and IR planning in a Databricks context beyond generic concepts.

## Key Concepts

- Unity Catalog RBAC and object-level permissions
- Tenant-scoped secret scopes and secret management
- Dynamic data masking and access controls for notebooks
- Audit logs and SIEM integration for detection
- Incident response playbooks and metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo policy enforcement in app layer
function authorize(user, dataset) {
  if (!user.tenants.includes(dataset.tenant)) throw new Error("Access denied");
  // additional masking rules could apply here
  return dataset;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test isolation guarantees across tenants? 
- What metrics and alerts would you instrument to detect leakage or misuse?
